% ============================================================================
% animal-babies — a familiar animal and the NAME OF ITS BABY (its young)
% (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the very first vocabulary lessons a small child learns: baby animals
% have SPECIAL NAMES. A baby dog is not a "baby dog" — it is a PUPPY. A baby cat
% is a KITTEN, a baby cow (cattle) is a CALF, a baby horse is a FOAL, a baby
% sheep is a LAMB, a baby kangaroo is a JOEY, and a baby goat is a KID. This
% library records, for a large set of familiar animals, what its young is
% called. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? animal_baby(dog, $Baby)            % puppy
%     ? animal_baby(kangaroo, $Baby)       % joey
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `animal_baby(animal, baby)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the baby's
% name WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on
% anything that is not one of the recorded animals (ask it about a `rock` and it
% stays honestly silent rather than inventing a name).
%
% The baby names, as a truth table over the animals shipped here:
%
%     animal    baby       a beginner's cue
%     --------  ---------  -------------------------------------------------
%     dog       puppy      a young dog; "pup" is the short form
%     cat       kitten     a young cat
%     cattle    calf       a young cow or bull (cattle) — also called a calf
%     horse     foal       a young horse (a colt if male, a filly if female)
%     sheep     lamb       a young sheep
%     kangaroo  joey       a young kangaroo, carried in its mother's pouch
%     goat      kid        a young goat — yes, the word "kid" comes from this!
%     bear      cub        a young bear
%     cheetah   cub        a young cheetah
%     deer      fawn       a young deer
%     eagle     eaglet     a young eagle
%     elephant  calf       a young elephant — same word as a baby cow
%     fox       kit        a young fox
%     frog      tadpole    the swimming, tailed larval stage of a frog
%     lion      cub        a young lion
%     owl       owlet      a young owl
%     penguin   chick      a young penguin
%     pig       piglet     a young pig
%     rabbit    bunny      a young rabbit
%     seal      pup        a young seal
%     swan      cygnet     a young swan
%     tiger     cub        a young tiger
%     whale     calf       a young whale — same word as a baby cow
%     wolf      cub        a young wolf
%
% The animals were chosen to be FAMILIAR and to have ONE clear, well-known baby
% name each. Note "cattle": the general word for cows and bulls is *cattle*, and
% that is the exact word the source uses, so we keep it as the animal here (a
% baby cow is still a calf). The relation is many-to-one on purpose — several
% animals genuinely share the same baby word in the source (calf for cattle,
% elephant, AND whale; cub for bear, cheetah, lion, tiger, AND wolf) — so the
% query also runs BACKWARD and can recall MORE than one animal for a shared
% baby word:
%
%     ? animal_baby($Animal, cub)   % bear ; cheetah ; lion ; tiger ; wolf
%
% A word that is NOT one of these animals — `rock`, `banana`, `oak` — is
% deliberately NOT a row, so a baby-name recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% fabricating a name. That honest-abstention property is what makes a recall
% table safe to build a reasoner on.
%
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (byte-quotable, per the ADJ-facts standard).
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Every row is copied from Wikipedia's "List of animal names" — the big table of
% animals with their male, female, YOUNG, and collective terms. Wikipedia is a
% general ENCYCLOPEDIA (a secondary reference), so the honest trust tier is
% `trust consensus` (not `authoritative`, which we reserve for a primary source
% like a museum, .gov or .edu). Nothing here is asserted from memory. Because ADJ
% tables carry ONE shared provenance envelope (per-row provenance is a documented
% future extension — see ADJ-TABLES §6), the envelope below cites the article at
% its `locator`, and the `source` quotes the HORSE row's "Young" cell verbatim
% (it contains "foal"). For full auditability, here is the exact "Young"-cell
% span that grounds EACH row, copied from that table:
%
%   dog       — "pup, puppy, whelp"                                  → puppy
%   cat       — "kit, kitten"                                        → kitten
%   cattle    — "calf"                                               → calf
%   horse     — "colt (male), filly (female), foal, weanling, ..."   → foal
%   sheep     — "cosset, lamb, lambkin"                              → lamb
%   kangaroo  — "joey"                                               → joey
%   goat      — "kid"                                                → kid
%   bear      — "cub"                                                → cub
%   cheetah   — "cub, kitten"                                        → cub
%   deer      — "calf, fawn"                                         → fawn
%   eagle     — "eaglet, fledgling"                                  → eaglet
%   elephant  — "calf"                                               → calf
%   fox       — "cub, kit, pup"                                      → kit
%   frog      — "froglet, polliwog, tadpole"                         → tadpole
%   lion      — "cub"                                                → cub
%   owl       — "fledgling, owlet"                                   → owlet
%   penguin   — "chick, nestling"                                    → chick
%   pig       — "farrow, piglet, shoat/shote (just weaned)"          → piglet
%   rabbit    — "bunny, kit, kitten, nestling"                       → bunny
%   seal      — "pup"                                                → pup
%   swan      — "cygnet, flapper"                                    → cygnet
%   tiger     — "cub, whelp"                                         → cub
%   whale     — "calf"                                               → calf
%   wolf      — "cub, pup, puppy, whelp"                             → cub
%
%   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_animal_names
%
% EXTENDED this cycle from 7 to 24 rows: the SAME already-cited Wikipedia "List
% of animal names" table names dozens more familiar animals; seventeen more were
% added here, each choosing the single most common, child-recognizable baby
% term from its "Young" cell (mirroring how the original seven rows already
% picked a generic term over a sex-specific or rare variant, e.g. horse's
% "foal" over "colt"/"filly"). WebFetch-verified with a targeted second pass
% re-fetching the raw "Young"-cell text for all seventeen new rows directly,
% confirming the extraction wasn't paraphrased.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table animal_baby {
    columns animal, baby

    row (dog, puppy)
    row (cat, kitten)
    row (cattle, calf)
    row (horse, foal)
    row (sheep, lamb)
    row (kangaroo, joey)
    row (goat, kid)
    row (bear, cub)
    row (cheetah, cub)
    row (deer, fawn)
    row (eagle, eaglet)
    row (elephant, calf)
    row (fox, kit)
    row (frog, tadpole)
    row (lion, cub)
    row (owl, owlet)
    row (penguin, chick)
    row (pig, piglet)
    row (rabbit, bunny)
    row (seal, pup)
    row (swan, cygnet)
    row (tiger, cub)
    row (whale, calf)
    row (wolf, cub)

    source "colt (male), filly (female), foal, weanling, yearling"
    locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_animal_names"
    trust consensus
}
